{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-compat","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The recovery helper introduces new grep, printf, and parameter-expansion patterns inside the script; verify Bash 3.2 portability and correct quoting/pattern handling for the four key names.","prompt_body":"Audit the new shell code added to write-design-current-env.sh for Bash 3.2 portability: check the helper function and recovery block for any bash 4+ constructs (associative arrays, namerefs, mapfile, ^^ case conversion, &>> redirection). Verify the grep pattern in recover_prior_env_value is correctly anchored and that the prefix-strip expansion \"${line#\"$prefix\"}\" round-trips cleanly through build_export output (printf '%q' of true/false produces bare tokens — confirm no trailing quote or escape character can survive the strip). Confirm the \"|| true\" guard after grep is inside the function body and not at the top level of a Bash tool block, consistent with BASH_AUTHORING.md §1. Confirm the grep call executes under a child bash process (real /usr/bin/grep) and is not exposed to the Bash-tool wrapper hazard described in BASH_AUTHORING.md §1. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-gap","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Case 13 and Case 14 cover preserve-on-refresh and single-side-explicit override, but the plan leaves untested what happens when both alias-pair members are supplied simultaneously with conflicting values, and whether the harness error-captures new case failures.","prompt_body":"Review the test plan in skills/design/scripts/test-write-design-current-env.sh: confirm Case 13 explicitly asserts MANUAL_REQUESTED is unset (not merely absent) after the no-flag refresh, distinguishing a missing key from a cleared one. Verify Case 14 covers the scenario where both --codex-present and --codex-available are supplied in the same call with conflicting values — the plan's pair-normalization logic only handles one-explicit/one-omitted, leaving the both-explicit-conflicting path implicit and potentially untested. Check whether the test harness's runner captures a non-zero exit code from each case so a silent script error cannot make a new case appear to pass. Confirm existing Cases 1-12 (especially Case 12's MANUAL_REQUESTED clear-on-omit assertion) are not structurally disrupted by any shared state or output-file reuse between cases. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"doc-sync","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan updates write-design-current-env.md but must also ensure no other files that document the four keys as clear-on-omit become stale, and that the sibling doc accurately describes pair-normalization not just simple recovery.","prompt_body":"Check whether the planned addition to write-design-current-env.md describes both pair-normalization (one explicitly-passed member propagates to the omitted peer) and simple recovery, not just the simpler recovery-only narrative. Search for any other files in skills/design/ or scripts/ that document CODEX_PRESENT, CURSOR_PRESENT, CODEX_AVAILABLE, or CURSOR_AVAILABLE as keys that are omitted or cleared on a no-flag refresh — if such references exist and are not updated by the plan, they will contradict the new preservation semantics. Verify the planned cross-reference to issue #3181 and harness Case 12 is placed in a location that operators reading the doc will encounter when looking up key-emit behavior, not buried in an unrelated section. Confirm the plan does not require updates to any SKILL.md step that explicitly documents these keys as absent after a refresh. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
